  "reason": "The records collectively make the later study plausibly reconstructible. R3, R4, and R9 already use simulated tensile deformation of the closely related CoNiCrFeMn FCC alloy and identify partial-dislocation processes. R6, R17, and R18 connect low stacking-fault energy, partial-dislocation-generated faults, and local FCC/HCP stacking changes, while R1, R2, and R19 demonstrate atomistic loading studies across temperature and very high strain-rate regimes. An expert could therefore reasonably extend this work to a bulk FeNiCrMn single crystal, compare crystallographic loading directions, temperatures, and strain rates, and expect Shockley-partial/stacking-fault-mediated plasticity with thermal softening. The exact role of vacancy clusters in nucleation, the negligible yield-rate sensitivity, the <-111> strength maximum, and the absence of twinning or bulk transformation are not directly anticipated, but they are specific findings rather than a substantial new intervention concept."
